main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ counts: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ more specialized: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
For tup<double>, the specialization for tup<F, R...> (R empty) did not
match: R stayed unassigned and the pattern was type-checked without
expanding R..., so tup<Ts...> was chosen.  Partial ordering also treated
an undeduced pack as a failure.  libstdc++ 11's variant then rejected
`std::variant<int, double> v = 2.5' (cpp17_probe_std_variant).
